## Why the AGPL and not a permissive licence

FBM asks members to put their commerce, their customer relationships and
their cooperative's internal ledger into shared infrastructure. The whole
basis for that ask is that they can leave with it. `docs/MEMBER_GOVERNANCE.md`
puts it as "a fork right that is not licensed is a norm, not a right", and
until this file existed the right was a norm.

Section 13 is the reason for this licence specifically rather than the GPL.
FBM is used over a network: a hosted marketplace is the normal way anyone
encounters it. Under a permissive licence, or under the plain GPL, an
operator could take this code, run it as a closed service, and owe its users
nothing — which is precisely the arrangement the project exists to be an
alternative to. Section 13 says that if you run a modified FBM as a network
service, the people using it can get your source. That is the same promise
FBM makes to its own members, applied to whoever comes next.

The cost is real and is accepted deliberately: code under this licence
cannot be absorbed into a closed product. An organisation that needs that
should ask about separate terms rather than assume them.


## Relationship to `docs/AGGRESSIVE_OPERATIONS_GUIDE.md`

That document states a preference for permissive licences and treats
strong-copyleft projects as sidecar-only. It is not in conflict with this
file: it governs what FBM may take *in* as a dependency, which is a
constraint imposed by the licence of the incoming code. This file governs
what FBM gives *out*. Choosing the AGPL for FBM's own code in fact relaxes
the inbound rule rather than tightening it, since an AGPL work can
incorporate GPL and AGPL sources that a permissively licensed FBM could not.


## Third-party software

FBM is built on, and distributed with, software by others. Their licences
are not superseded by this one — each component remains under its own terms,
and the copyright notices in `node_modules` are part of the distribution.

  - MedusaJS (`@medusajs/*`) — MIT — https://github.com/medusajs/medusa
  - Mercur (`@mercurjs/*`) — MIT — https://github.com/mercurjs/mercur
  - Stellar SDK (`@stellar/stellar-sdk`) — Apache-2.0
  - lightningcss — MPL-2.0 (redistribution under the AGPL is permitted by
    MPL-2.0 section 3.3)
  - the remainder of the dependency tree — predominantly MIT, Apache-2.0,
    ISC and BSD

As of this writing the tree contains no GPL, AGPL, SSPL, BUSL or
Elastic-licensed package, and no dependency's terms conflict with
distributing the combined work under the AGPL. Apache-2.0 is compatible in
this direction only: Apache-2.0 code may be combined into an AGPL-3.0 work,
but AGPL-3.0 code may not be taken back into an Apache-2.0 or MIT project.

FBM began from the Mercur marketplace starter, and some files in
`backend/`, `admin-panel/` and `vendor-panel/` still derive from Medusa and
Mercur sources. Those upstream projects are MIT-licensed, which permits
relicensing the combined work, and this notice preserves the attribution
that the MIT licence requires. `backend/package.json` declared
`"license": "MIT"` until this change; that field was the Medusa starter
template's own metadata, left unedited along with its `medusa-starter-default`
package name. It was never a licence chosen for FBM.


## Open questions for the operator

These are recorded rather than guessed at, and should be settled before the
first outside contribution lands, because each becomes harder afterwards.

  1. **The copyright holder.** "Blackmarket Coalition" is the project's name,
     not necessarily a legal entity. If no entity has been formed, copyright
     vests in the individual authors and this line should name them; once one
     is formed, assign to it and update this file. The repository history
     shows commits from more than one address — anyone who is a separate
     person is a co-holder whose agreement is needed to relicense.

  2. **Whether to offer commercial terms.** Dual licensing (AGPL for the
     commons, a paid exception for anyone who needs to embed FBM in a closed
     product) is compatible with everything above and is a normal revenue
     path. It requires holding all the copyright, which is the same
     precondition as (1). No commercial licence is offered here, because
     offering one means naming a seller and a contact. Note that Blackout's
     `LICENSE-COMMERCIAL` is Element's, inherited unmodified from the Synapse
     fork and pointing at `licensing@element.io`; it is not a coalition
     licence and is not a template to copy.

  3. **A contributor agreement.** Without a CLA or DCO, outside contributions
     arrive under the AGPL and cannot be relicensed without each
     contributor's consent. That is a defensible position — it is what keeps
     the licence honest — but it forecloses (2) for any code written by
     someone else, so it is a choice to make knowingly rather than by
     default.
